[#:seon.task{:name "a-cancelled-future-is-not-exit"
             :kind :function
             :intent "stop-work-launcher! cancels each Future and settles it at once, so 'drained' can mean the callback emitted while work-fn is still inside a host call. The launcher lifecycle atoms (atom audit A100-A105) fold into the submission map together with this actual-exit distinction (15-35 lines, reviewed)."
             :must-read #{seon.flow/stop-work-launcher!}
             :evidence #{"docs/research/agent-platform/landing/standing-deletion-2026-09-26.md"
                         "docs/seon/issues/cancelled-future-is-not-body-exit-evidence.md"}}
 #:seon.task{:name "blocking-konserve-io-bursts-the-mixed-pool-to-five-hundred-threads"
             :kind :function
             :intent "The core.async :mixed pool bursts to about 500 threads from blocking konserve IO. Done when konserve IO runs on virtual threads (our fork) and the thread count under the same load probe is bounded, measured before and after. Post-restart 2026-10-07 (default 108dd1c3e): runtime_status 881 platform threads, 424 of 528 idle async-mixed workers (ThreadPoolExecutor.getTask, 60 s keepalive), largest pool 751, decaying to 35 when quiet: a burst, not a leak. The :mixed pool is also :core-async-dispatch (core.async 1.10.874-alpha3 dispatch.clj:98-111, newCachedThreadPool, unbounded), and konserve filestore async mode runs blocking file calls inside go blocks (filestore.clj:153-158 -create-blob/-delete-blob, defaults.cljc:237 delete-blob), so each concurrent store call pins a dispatch thread: 269 busy at once during a whole-store collection, ~720 dispatches per ordinary eval_clj. Smallest producer fix to evaluate: a clojure.core.async.executor-factory system property returning a virtual-thread executor for :mixed/:core-async-dispatch, or the konserve fork moving those calls to io-thread."
             :must-read #{seon.cluster.store/datahike-configuration}}
 #:seon.task{:name "graph-report-channels-hold-whole-pass-reports"
             :kind :function
             :intent "Each graph's core.async.flow report channel has a sliding buffer of 100 and Seon puts each whole pass report there (`seon.turn/step` emits {::flow/report [report]}, `seon.flow/work-launcher-step` each work ::value) while nothing reads the report or error channel `seon.flow/start-graph!` returns: one channel held 58 reports of about 590 KB each, 34.3 MB, growing with use per graph with no dial (heap-live lane MAT on a full-GC dump of default, 2026-10-08, live set 1.16-1.31 GB). Channels carry only rederivable or replaceable data: reports carry ids and timings only. Done when a graph's report channel retains under 1 KB per report after a pass."
             :must-read #{seon.flow/start-graph! seon.flow/work-launcher-step seon.turn/step}}

 #:seon.task{:name "forced-child-jvm-death-fixture-write-is-refused-for-its-projection"
             :kind :function
             :intent "The declared-long `seon.flow-test/forced-child-jvm-death-preserves-committed-facts` is red on d9eb15c05 and on its child: the child JVM's `seon.test-support/transacted!` is refused with 'This operation requires a carried schema projection' (`seon.db/carried-projection` falls back), so the child exits 1 before committing its fact and the test also exceeds its 5 s bound. Done when the child's fixture write carries its projection and the test is green under `bin/seon check --include-long` (run fc5cadd8c1bc)."
             :must-read #{seon.test-support/transacted! seon.db/carried-projection}}
 #:seon.task{:name "a-step-var-defined-in-sci-cannot-be-built-into-a-proc"
             :kind :function
             :intent "`seon.flow/var-process` refuses a step Var defined at an agent's REPL, so a flow test of a real proc step cannot be authored there. Repro (default caba650fa, agent chain-frictions-probe, 2026-10-10, SCI): (ns cf.scratch) (defn cf-step [state in] [state nil]) (seon.flow/var-process #'cf.scratch/cf-step :compute {:seon.env/environment {}}) answers \"refused argument 0 `step-var`: expected :seon.flow/step-var (must be a Var so a running graph hot reloads its step), got an instance of sci.lang.Var\" (the first call with two arguments is an arity refusal, a separate and correct answer). The `:seon.flow/step-var` schema accepts a host Var only, and `var-process` resolves it for hot reload; an SCI agent's definitions are program entities in its context. Done when a step Var the agent's context holds builds a launcher whose graph runs that definition (and hot reloads it), or the refusal names the supported way to test a step from the REPL, with a regression that defines a step in SCI and runs one proc step through `seon.flow/var-process`."
             :must-read #{seon.flow/var-process seon.flow/start-graph! seon.sci.lookup/agent-test-namespace}}]
